[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609076000-the-apex-workers-leave-the-app"
  :adr/title "The four Workers that own hostnames leave the app; the one that owns none stays"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-07"
  :adr/related "adr-2609075600-the-cli-leaves-the-app"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

itonami.cloud の repo を cloud-itonami-app / -cli / -api / -apex（+ -auth）に
整理（オーナー、2026-09-07）。Second of the four, after `cloud-itonami-cli`.

## The line was measured, not chosen

The question for `-apex` looked like a taste question -- which Workers count as
edge? -- so it was answered the same way the CLI seam was: by counting
references into the app tree, excluding comments.

    agent-edge               0
    itonami-cloud-webhooks   0
    itonami-fleet-dispatch   0
    mcp-edge                 0
    app-edge                 4

Four move. `app-edge` stays, and two facts make that a measurement rather than
a preference:

1. **Its dependency is in production code, not a test.**
   `services/app-edge/src/cloud/itonami/edge/worker.cljs` requires
   `cloud.itonami.app.fleet-core` and `cloud.itonami.app.kotoba-oracle`. The CLI
   case turned on a consumer that had no launcher; this one ships.
2. **`kotoba-oracle` is shared foundation.** Six other app namespaces require it
   (`did-web`, `session-handoff`, `oauth-resource`, `policy.kotoba`,
   `health-core.kotoba`, `fleet-core.kotoba`). It is not edge code that happens
   to live in the app.

`fleet-core` additionally has a `.kotoba` twin under an active parity test.
Splitting a namespace in the middle of a Kotoba migration would fork it.

## The boundary was already drawn by the hostnames

The four independent Workers own `agent.` / `hooks.` / `app.` / `mcp.`
itonami.cloud. `app-edge` owns none -- its wrangler config says, in its own
comment, that it deploys to workers.dev only so that a slice in progress cannot
take a live host with it.

So the coupled Worker is exactly the one with nothing live to lose, and the
four with real hosts are exactly the four that were already independent. The
split did not have to impose a boundary; it had to find the one that was there.

## Verified by building, not by copying

Each Worker was built from the new tree with `wrangler deploy --dry-run` before
the repository existed. All four returned 0.

That check was then shown to discriminate: appending invalid JavaScript to
`agent-edge/src/index.js` returned exit 1 with `Build failed`, and restoring it
returned 0. A build check that cannot fail is not evidence that a move worked.
`agent-edge`'s own nbb suite also passes in the new tree.

## One thing removed on the way

`services/itonami-fleet-dispatch/.wrangler/cache/wrangler-account.json` came
along with the copy. It holds a Cloudflare account id and name -- not a
credential, and **already tracked in the public `cloud-itonami-app`
repository**, so moving it would have exposed nothing new. It is local wrangler
cache, so it was dropped here and `.wrangler/` is gitignored instead.

It is still tracked upstream. That is a small hygiene item in
`cloud-itonami-app`, not fixed here, and recorded so it is not lost.

## What has NOT happened

Nothing is deleted from `cloud-itonami-app`. Both trees carry these four
Workers, and the live deployments still run from wherever they were last
deployed -- moving source does not move a Worker. Cutting over means deploying
each one from this repository and confirming the host still answers, which is a
separate change per Worker so that a rollback stays per-Worker.

`-auth` and `-api` are still not started, for the reasons in
adr-2609075400: `-auth` needs a decision about what the app asks it FOR, and
`-api` needs `server.clj` split into routes and handlers first."}]
